1
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:05,840
Hi everyone, my name is
Dr. Joseph Holloway and

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:08,120
in this session we're
going to talk about viral

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:11,040
infections associated with
the respiratory system.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,320
So here are some
of the learning

5
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,300
objectives for
the lecture today.

6
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:21,460
For each virus we'll be
discussing some of its

7
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:25,100
properties, clinical
presentation, pathogenesis,

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,400
and we'll consider
the epidemiology

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,080
associated with each. So

10
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:36,230
there's several viruses
that cause respiratory

11
00:00:36,230 --> 00:00:39,250
infections, and here's the
list that we'll be focusing

12
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:41,790
on, which covers most
of the major examples.

13
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:48,070
Now, before we get started,
it's important to understand

14
00:00:48,070 --> 00:00:51,150
modes of transmission when
it comes to microbiology.

15
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:54,230
So I just wanted to briefly
make note of it, because

16
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:57,270
many of the viruses we'll
discuss here are contagious,

17
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:00,750
and prevention, in part,
involves combating their

18
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:04,770
modes of transmission. So,
respiratory infections are

19
00:01:04,770 --> 00:01:08,150
associated with both broad
categories of transmission.

20
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:11,690
Direct transmission
includes large droplet

21
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,190
transmission, like a
sneeze or a cough, but also

22
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:18,390
involves immediate
person-to-person contact.

23
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:21,870
While indirect
transmission occurs when a

24
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:25,570
pathogen is carried to
a susceptible person by

25
00:01:25,570 --> 00:01:28,010
way of different
materials or substances,

26
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:31,330
like contaminated objects
or food and water.

27
00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:34,530
And this is also where
we primarily find

28
00:01:34,530 --> 00:01:36,910
fecal-oral contamination
and transmission.

29
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:41,090
Indirect can also be through
aerosols. Small particles

30
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:44,350
can travel long distances
in the air. And indirect

31
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:48,450
can also be by way of vectors
like ticks or mosquitoes.

32
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:48,970
So

33
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,260
the first thing to mention
here is about viral

34
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:59,140
classification, which is
primarily based on the

35
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:03,480
viral genome, as being
either DNA or RNA-based,

36
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,160
and then single-stranded
or double-stranded.

37
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,060
Single-stranded RNA
viruses are also described

38
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:12,160
by the orientation
of their genetic

39
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:15,100
material, either positive
sense or negative.

40
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,160
Now, classification
can also extend

41
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:20,700
to things like
capsid morphology,

42
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:22,880
the presence of an envelope, as

43
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,820
well as the host
range for a virus.

44
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,200
So I'll mention the
basic classification

45
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:29,300
of each virus as
we go along here.

46
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,800
All right, so let's start with

47
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,880
the first family of virus.

48
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,100
Pocorinoviruses are
small, non-enveloped,

49
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:42,180
positive-sense, single
-stranded RNA viruses.

50
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:47,200
They all have four major
proteins, which are named VP1

51
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,900
through 4, and those are
used to produce the capsid.

52
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:56,000
There are two main genera
of Pocorinoviruses, which

53
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,300
are known as enteroviruses
and rhinoviruses, and they

54
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:04,060
both have slightly different
properties. So, enteroviruses

55
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:08,780
are more prevalent in the
summer and fall, whereas

56
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:12,560
rhinoviruses are more
prevalent from fall to spring.

57
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:16,240
Now, the enteroviruses
do cause a very

58
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:19,620
small number of general
colds in adults,

59
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:22,480
specifically the non
-polio enteroviruses,

60
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:25,580
but it's really
rhinoviruses that make up

61
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,840
the largest percentage
of the common cold.

62
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:31,140
And they also come in a whole

63
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:32,640
host of different serotypes.

64
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,640
Rhinoviruses also replicate
at a slightly lower

65
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,160
temperature, which happens
to match the cooler

66
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,580
temperature inside the
human nasal cavity.

67
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:46,240
And they are very easily
destroyed in low pH

68
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,780
environments like stomach
acid, so they don't

69
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:52,020
typically infect the GI
tract like enteroviruses do.

70
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:57,180
whereas enteroviruses
have usually a more robust

71
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:00,480
protein capsid, and that
allows them to resist things

72
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,880
like bile salts and the
acidic environment found

73
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:07,680
in the GI tract, or even
in sewage and wastewater.

74
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:11,540
In the group of
enteroviruses, we'll

75
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,320
only focus here on
Coxsackieviruses,

76
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:18,160
specifically Coxsackievirus
A, which is most

77
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:20,960
associated with hand
-foot-mouth disease. There

78
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,760
are two main categories of

79
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:31,720
Coxsackievirus,
labeled A and B.

80
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:34,920
Now B is more associated
with heart conditions like

81
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:39,780
myocarditis and pericarditis,
whereas Coxsackievirus A

82
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:42,840
is associated with a number
of different conditions.

83
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:46,840
So things like hemorrhagic
conjunctivitis,

84
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:49,040
aseptic meningitis,

85
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:53,300
herpangina, which is a
type of pharyngitis with

86
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,560
sores in the back of the
mouth and the throat along

87
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:59,000
with a fever, and of
course, most commonly hand

88
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,920
-foot-mouth disease, which
is generally going to be

89
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:07,020
caused by serotypes A16
and A6. Now, for this, the

90
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:09,880
virus generally enters
the mouth. It's going to

91
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,620
replicate in the pharynx
and the lower intestine,

92
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:15,740
and then it disseminates
through the bloodstream.

93
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,880
Due to the viremia,

94
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:25,410
hand-foot-mouth disease
presents with a fever

95
00:05:25,410 --> 00:05:29,820
usually painful oral lesions
and a vesicular rash on

96
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:32,900
the hands and the feet
the rash is usually going

97
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:36,160
to heal without crusting
and that will clinically

98
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:39,620
differentiate it from
vesicles that you would see

99
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:44,340
caused by herpes viruses
or pox viruses now the

100
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:47,520
virus here can be shed for
long periods of time and

101
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:50,840
so the fecal oral contamination
route is one of the

102
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:54,140
more common modes of
transmission and explains

103
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:57,920
why outbreaks are usually
seen in daycare centers,

104
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:00,580
schools, summer
camps, and other

105
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:02,100
similar settings like that.

106
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:05,740
So proper hygiene is especially

107
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:08,060
important to prevent
disease transmission,

108
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:10,440
specifically with
small children.

109
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:14,380
A diagnosis of this
can usually be based on

110
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:17,240
clinical symptoms, but
if necessary, it can

111
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,340
also be identified with
molecular techniques,

112
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:22,380
and the treatment is
usually just supportive.

113
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:30,600
Okay, let's switch here
to rhinoviruses, which are

114
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:33,840
one of the most common
etiologies of the common cold.

115
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:38,100
Infections occur frequently
by self-inoculation, which

116
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,000
is going to happen when
you touch a contaminated

117
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,820
surface, or maybe the hand
of an infected person, and

118
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:46,760
then you transfer the virus
directly to your own nose,

119
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:51,080
mouth, or eyes. Within a
few days after exposure, the

120
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:54,100
virus can actually be
detected in nasal secretions,

121
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:59,020
while overall viral shedding
can continue for weeks.

122
00:07:00,290 --> 00:07:02,580
Replication is going to
be limited to the nasal

123
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:05,900
mucosa, in part due to the
temperature constraints

124
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:09,840
that we talked about. And
in order to infect cells,

125
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,360
the rhinoviruses use a
receptor known as ICAM

126
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:17,340
-1, which is the intracellular
adhesion molecule

127
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:21,420
1 receptor found on the
surface of epithelial

128
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:24,200
cells, and it uses
that in order to dock.

129
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:27,780
Now, the infection
then induces local

130
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:31,640
inflammation, and inflammation
then upregulates the

131
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:34,880
expression of ICAM-1,
that docking protein.

132
00:07:35,630 --> 00:07:39,060
Now, in general, we view
children as the primary

133
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:42,620
vectors for rhinoviruses,
and that's because they

134
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:45,220
generally have less
developed immune systems.

135
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:49,180
They typically lack exposure
to many of the strains

136
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:52,880
and serotypes of the virus,
and also because they tend

137
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:57,680
to practice very poor
hygiene in very close contact

138
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:00,860
environments like you see
in schools and daycares.

139
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:02,920
So this creates a
perfect situation

140
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,040
for infection and transmission.

141
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:12,480
The acute illness lasts
for about a week, although

142
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:15,260
some symptoms can linger
for a bit longer than that.

143
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:20,120
In adults, they commonly
present with sneezing,

144
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:23,260
some nasal discharge, a
sore throat, headache,

145
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:26,120
you know, just general
fatigue and malaise.

146
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:29,480
Usually there's no fever
in adults, but generally

147
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:32,280
there is in children.
And you would also

148
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:34,580
want to consider any sort
of secondary infection

149
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:37,260
or complication that
may produce a case of

150
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:40,940
bronchitis or pneumonia,
especially in children.

151
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,180
A diagnosis, again, is
usually clinical, but

152
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:49,840
you could also use a viral
panel or some molecular

153
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:52,880
technique to identify
it. And treatment,

154
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,560
like any common cold,
would just be supportive.

155
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:02,540
Okay, so we'll switch to
a different group, the

156
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:06,640
adenoviruses, which are non
-envelope, double-stranded DNA

157
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,520
viruses that usually produce
subclinical infections.

158
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:14,480
But they are also known
to cause a whole host of

159
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:17,820
conditions, like you can see
here in the table, depending

160
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:20,480
on the patient population
that you're talking about.

161
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:25,000
Now, the virus
replicates in the mucosal

162
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,180
epithelium of many areas
of the body, like the eye,

163
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:32,460
the respiratory tract,
the GI tract, and the

164
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:35,620
urinary tract, but they
usually don't spread

165
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:38,160
much further beyond the
regional lymph nodes.

166
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:42,680
The most common serotypes
are the low-numbered

167
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:46,870
respiratory types, so things
like 1, 2, and 3, while

168
00:09:46,870 --> 00:09:49,680
there are other serotypes
that are associated with

169
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:54,460
specific conditions like
serotype 40 and 41 that are

170
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:57,580
enteric and are associated
with gastroenteritis.

171
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:05,560
Adenoviruses are spread
in a number of different

172
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:08,680
ways by both direct
and indirect contact.

173
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:12,140
The incubation period for
adenovirus infection is

174
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:15,980
anywhere from a few days
to two weeks, with viral

175
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:20,160
shedding after infection
lasting up to a month and a

176
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:23,780
half, depending on the
person's age and immune status.

177
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:27,620
Now, infection with
adenovirus begins with its

178
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:31,760
attachment, and it uses a
protein fiber for initial

179
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:34,900
connection to a cell
receptor known as the

180
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,680
CAR receptor, the Coxsackie
adenovirus receptor.

181
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:43,400
And it then uses a
pentamer that holds

182
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,700
that protein fiber
to then mediate

183
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:49,300
internalization by way
of cellular integrins.

184
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:54,220
Now, it's those pentons
and hexons that are

185
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:57,260
going to be components of
the viral capsid. Those

186
00:10:57,260 --> 00:11:00,520
are both capsomere units
of the viral capsid.

187
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:05,180
adenoviruses undergo
two basic stages of

188
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:09,640
transcription during the
replication cycle early on

189
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:12,740
non-structural regulatory
proteins are going to

190
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:15,480
be synthesized that tend
to promote cell growth

191
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,340
and then late transcription
is what produces

192
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,700
material required for
viral particle production

193
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:29,720
Infections with
adenovirus can cause many

194
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:32,900
different issues, anything
from pharyngitis to

195
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:35,580
pneumonia, but usually
it's just going to

196
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:38,540
produce generalized
symptoms like a cough and

197
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:41,540
nasal discharge, fever,
and a sore throat.

198
00:11:41,920 --> 00:11:44,560
Now, there can be
ocular involvement as

199
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:47,280
well, which can lead
to very particular

200
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:50,420
conditions like
pharyngeoconjunctival fever.

201
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:54,980
The viruses are present
all year round, but

202
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:57,640
sometimes outbreaks
can occur in a common

203
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:00,540
setting, like many
children getting swimming

204
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:03,100
pool conjunctivitis at
a camp, for instance.

205
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:08,080
Adenoviruses are also
the cause of epidemics in

206
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:11,660
military recruits, with
vaccines being used previously

207
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:16,000
in that setting, but were
not publicly available.

208
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:20,360
Generally speaking, hand
washing and hygiene is

209
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:22,900
probably the easiest way
to prevent infections,

210
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:27,980
and if detection of
adenovirus is required, that

211
00:12:27,980 --> 00:12:31,380
could be accomplished just
by PCR or immunoassays.

212
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:40,020
okay let's switch to the
orthomyxoviruses which are

213
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:44,400
enveloped negative sense
single-stranded RNA viruses

214
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:48,460
whose genome is segmented
which is an important

215
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:51,920
feature now the main
representative here is going to

216
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:57,160
be the influenza virus and
the key to its epidemic

217
00:12:57,160 --> 00:13:01,880
potential relates to that
segmented viral genome, which

218
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:05,500
allows for genetic
reassortment when a cell is

219
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:08,900
infected by more than one
strain of the flu virus,

220
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:13,340
leading to a very high rate
of natural variation by way

221
00:13:13,340 --> 00:13:17,760
of antigenic shift that
can rapidly produce new

222
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:22,020
strains of the flu virus
that contain hybrid genomes.

223
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:26,900
Now, antigenic shift only
occurs in the influenza

224
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:31,460
type A category. In
addition to humans, type A

225
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:36,640
flu viruses can also infect
wild birds and landfowl,

226
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:39,300
which act as a reservoir
for the flu virus,

227
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:44,020
and swine, so animals
like pigs and hogs. They

228
00:13:44,020 --> 00:13:47,460
usually act as the mixing
vessel for flu strains.

229
00:13:47,930 --> 00:13:50,660
Type B and C on
the other hand are

230
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:54,180
almost exclusively
infective to humans.

231
00:13:54,940 --> 00:13:58,040
Now another way for a
virus to change is through

232
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:01,640
antigenic drift which
is a slower process of

233
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:05,780
randomized mutations over
time that result in minor

234
00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:09,700
changes to a strain and
that's a feature that you see

235
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:12,780
in all three different
types of flu categories.

236
00:14:13,100 --> 00:14:16,880
Now the types are actually
divided A, B, and C

237
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:19,860
by way of their structural
protein differences.

238
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:23,420
But the antigenic
variants and the different

239
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:26,760
strains that we see in
group A are due instead

240
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:30,540
to different versions
of two surface proteins,

241
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:34,420
hemagglutinin and
neuraminidase, which get

242
00:14:34,420 --> 00:14:37,920
numbered independently
and provides the subtype

243
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:42,200
for group A viruses
like H5N1 for instance.

244
00:14:42,940 --> 00:14:45,080
So here we're going to focus

245
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:47,540
on type A influenza virus.

246
00:14:51,430 --> 00:14:53,780
Transmission of
the flu occurs via

247
00:14:53,780 --> 00:14:57,320
contact with large
droplets and aerosols

248
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:01,440
as well as fomites and
sometimes we see seasonal

249
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:04,700
epidemics in the winter
months. The incubation

250
00:15:04,700 --> 00:15:08,820
period for the flu is
usually a few days, but viral

251
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:12,160
shedding can actually
start before symptoms and

252
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:16,420
can last around 10 days
after symptoms begin, which

253
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:19,100
really contributes to its
high transmissibility.

254
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:23,320
The infection cycle of
the flu is mediated by the

255
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:26,380
two surface glycoproteins
that we just mentioned.

256
00:15:26,380 --> 00:15:31,440
So hemagglutinin initiates
infection by binding

257
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:35,120
to sialic acid on host
cells, which allows entry.

258
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:38,800
And then neuraminidase
is going to cleave

259
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:42,040
sialic acid, releasing
new virions from the

260
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:45,340
host cell, and also
prevents viral clumping.

261
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:47,840
While at the same time,
it alters the properties

262
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:50,540
of the airway by thinning
the mucus barrier.

263
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:58,920
Patients typically experience
the abrupt onset of

264
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:03,120
symptoms, like fever,
headache, and dry cough, and

265
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:06,340
those systemic symptoms
usually last about a week.

266
00:16:06,820 --> 00:16:11,040
Children often have similar
symptoms to adults, but

267
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:13,820
generally they'll have
higher fevers and they might

268
00:16:13,820 --> 00:16:17,460
also have the addition of
gastrointestinal involvement,

269
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:22,300
so things like diarrhea
or nausea and vomiting.

270
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:26,900
Now, diagnosis of the flu is
usually going to be clinical

271
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:30,620
for uncomplicated cases,
but of course, PCR could

272
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:34,400
be used to confirm or to
discriminate between viruses.

273
00:16:35,370 --> 00:16:38,980
Now, complications of the
flu can range a bit, so

274
00:16:38,980 --> 00:16:42,740
it could be anything from
pneumonia or myocarditis,

275
00:16:42,740 --> 00:16:47,300
encephalitis and sepsis,
or croup, which you

276
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:50,960
can see here in the x
-ray showing the classic

277
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:55,240
steeple sign, which is the
swelling of the larynx.

278
00:16:55,880 --> 00:16:58,900
Prevention strategies,
of course, are going

279
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:01,560
to include hand and
respiratory hygiene,

280
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:04,080
but certainly
seasonal vaccination

281
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:06,640
is a primary means
of prevention.

282
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,300
Neuraminidase inhibitors
can be used for

283
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:14,060
treatment of the flu as well,
but should be administered

284
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:16,880
early in the infection
process, usually

285
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:20,180
within about 48 hours
of when symptoms occur.

286
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:27,040
Paramyxoviruses are
enveloped, negative

287
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:29,540
sense, single
-stranded RNA viruses

288
00:17:29,540 --> 00:17:32,560
that fall into two
basic subgroups.

289
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:36,320
There's the paramyxoviridae,
which includes

290
00:17:36,320 --> 00:17:40,360
viruses that cause
parainfluenza, as well

291
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:43,140
as causing other conditions
like the mumps and

292
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:45,840
measles that we won't
be discussing here.

293
00:17:46,330 --> 00:17:48,760
And then there's the
pneumoviridae that

294
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:52,800
includes RSV and
human metanumovirus.

295
00:17:53,810 --> 00:17:57,060
Now, members of this
whole viral family will

296
00:17:57,060 --> 00:18:01,860
initiate infection via the
respiratory tract. And the

297
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:05,500
virions have two
glycoproteins on their surface

298
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:08,680
that form the major antigenic
targets for neutralizing

299
00:18:08,680 --> 00:18:12,340
antibodies. Now one
of those proteins is a

300
00:18:12,340 --> 00:18:16,700
fusion protein and the
other glycoprotein possesses

301
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:20,420
hemagglutinin and or
neuraminidase activities.

302
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:24,300
So here we're going to
discuss parainfluenza

303
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:27,100
and RSV since
those are prevalent

304
00:18:27,100 --> 00:18:29,520
infections that cause
respiratory disease.

305
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:36,900
So the most severe
clinical manifestations of

306
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:39,920
parainfluenza virus are
going to be seen in children.

307
00:18:40,060 --> 00:18:42,920
For most adults,
it would generally

308
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:45,240
just present like
a common cold.

309
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:50,180
Parainfluenza virus is
fairly ubiquitous and

310
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:53,980
infection with it is common,
generally through the nose

311
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:57,980
or the eyes and is most
frequently associated with

312
00:18:57,980 --> 00:19:01,820
daycare and early school
settings now there are

313
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:04,750
four different subtypes
of parainfluenza virus

314
00:19:04,750 --> 00:19:08,800
types 1 through 3 cause
lower respiratory tract

315
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:13,620
infections with type 1 and
2 also being the major cause

316
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:18,240
of croup which is a horse
barking cough that can

317
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:21,040
lead to throat swelling,
just like we saw before.

318
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,020
Now, type 4 generally is
going to be milder and is

319
00:19:27,020 --> 00:19:30,420
typically associated with
upper respiratory infections.

320
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:35,600
In general, the extent
of infection usually is

321
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:39,080
going to correlate with
the disease severity. So

322
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:42,020
some infections can
spread lower and can cause

323
00:19:42,020 --> 00:19:46,900
pneumonia or bronchiolitis
again diagnosis depends

324
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:50,920
typically on clinical
presentation but of course

325
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:54,460
pcr could be used as a
diagnostic method as well

326
00:19:54,780 --> 00:19:58,240
and treatment is primarily
going to be supportive

327
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:04,240
okay so we'll move on to the

328
00:20:04,240 --> 00:20:07,360
other subgroup and discuss RSV,

329
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:11,380
which stands for
respiratory syncytial virus.

330
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:16,060
And even though this is a
pretty severe health threat

331
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:20,140
to the elderly and the
immunocompromised, we're

332
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:23,180
going to focus basically on
infants and children here.

333
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:28,560
Now, RSV is the most
important cause of lower

334
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:31,700
respiratory tract illness
in children, and it

335
00:20:31,700 --> 00:20:34,580
infects virtually everybody
by the age of two,

336
00:20:34,580 --> 00:20:38,500
followed by reinfection
throughout your lifetime.

337
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:42,620
It's spread in a multitude
of different ways

338
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:46,280
by direct and indirect
contact and it can

339
00:20:46,280 --> 00:20:49,060
also survive on surfaces
for up to six hours

340
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:52,200
before entering into a
person's nose or eyes.

341
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:56,480
RSV also is known
to cause seasonal

342
00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:59,500
outbreaks that typically
are going to be seen

343
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:02,520
between fall and
springtime in the U.S.

344
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:07,060
Now, mortality is
generally uncommon in

345
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:10,380
resource abundant
countries like ours, but

346
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:14,440
RSV remains an extremely
important cause of

347
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:16,640
death for infants and
children worldwide.

348
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,540
Now, RSV infection
begins with viral entry

349
00:21:21,540 --> 00:21:24,400
into the upper respiratory
tract where it's

350
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:27,460
going to infect airway
epithelial cells.

351
00:21:28,140 --> 00:21:32,820
It then spreads cell-to
-cell, forming syncytia, which

352
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:36,580
are basically merged
cells. And it does this by

353
00:21:36,580 --> 00:21:41,720
utilizing its fusion protein
and another attachment surface

354
00:21:41,720 --> 00:21:45,320
glycoprotein to be able
to enter cells and then

355
00:21:45,320 --> 00:21:48,920
fuse those neighboring
infected cells together, which

356
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:52,370
creates large multinucleated
structures that are called

357
00:21:52,370 --> 00:21:55,340
syncytia. And that's where
it gets its name from.

358
00:21:55,600 --> 00:22:00,080
Now, the virus and the
immune system cause

359
00:22:00,080 --> 00:22:03,840
cell death, excessive
mucus production, and

360
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:07,400
airway inflammation,
which then leads to airway

361
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:10,700
obstruction. So some
of the pathological

362
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:13,580
effect here is initiated
by the immune system.

363
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:21,720
The spectrum of respiratory
illnesses caused by RSV

364
00:22:21,720 --> 00:22:27,160
in infants can range anywhere
from a common cold to

365
00:22:27,160 --> 00:22:30,790
more severe lower respiratory
tract infections like

366
00:22:30,790 --> 00:22:34,960
bronchiolitis, which is
the most common clinical

367
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:39,780
syndrome associated with
RSV in infants, and that

368
00:22:39,780 --> 00:22:43,740
usually presents as wheezing
with a low-grade fever.

369
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:47,680
RSV infection usually
is self-limited,

370
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:50,280
but it's also been
associated with

371
00:22:50,280 --> 00:22:52,560
recurrent symptoms
in some patients.

372
00:22:54,220 --> 00:22:58,360
Diagnosing RSV is generally
based on clinical and

373
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:01,860
epidemiological features,
but you could also use

374
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:06,640
nasal secretions with a
rapid antigen test or for PCR

375
00:23:06,640 --> 00:23:11,720
analysis. And actually,
multiplex PCR assays for

376
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:15,280
detection of RSV, along with
other respiratory viruses

377
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:18,220
like the flu, are available
in most hospitals.

378
00:23:19,300 --> 00:23:22,140
The treatment is
going to be primarily

379
00:23:22,140 --> 00:23:25,000
supportive for both
children and adults.

380
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:29,360
For young children at high
risk, there are antiviral

381
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:32,500
treatments, which include
monoclonal antibodies.

382
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:36,820
You could vaccinate
pregnant women, which is

383
00:23:36,820 --> 00:23:41,660
going to provide passive
immunity for a baby, as

384
00:23:41,660 --> 00:23:45,080
well as administering
a preventative antibody

385
00:23:45,080 --> 00:23:48,480
shot during the first
RSV season after birth.

386
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:57,080
okay so we'll shift here
to our last viral example

387
00:23:57,080 --> 00:24:01,900
the coronaviruses which
are enveloped positive

388
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:07,180
sense single-stranded RNA
viruses now coronaviruses

389
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:12,560
are and have been
ideological agents of the

390
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:16,260
common cold, and there
are representatives from

391
00:24:16,260 --> 00:24:21,380
both the alpha and beta
groups of coronaviruses,

392
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:25,000
although we have recently
become much more familiar

393
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:28,280
with the last three beta
serotypes listed here.

394
00:24:29,300 --> 00:24:32,980
Now, similar to type
A influenza viruses,

395
00:24:33,620 --> 00:24:37,580
coronaviruses are zoonotic,
meaning that they are

396
00:24:37,580 --> 00:24:40,540
naturally transmitted
between animals and humans,

397
00:24:40,660 --> 00:24:42,920
which has certainly
been the case for the

398
00:24:42,920 --> 00:24:47,480
viruses associated with
SARS, MERS, and COVID-19.

399
00:24:51,140 --> 00:24:56,300
So we'll focus here on
SARS-CoV-2, which currently

400
00:24:56,300 --> 00:24:59,820
is associated with many
asymptomatic infections,

401
00:25:00,040 --> 00:25:04,540
but is still highly
contagious. And infected

402
00:25:04,540 --> 00:25:08,360
individuals can shed virus
for up to a week and a half.

403
00:25:08,760 --> 00:25:11,180
Now, the most important
mode of disease

404
00:25:11,180 --> 00:25:13,840
transmission is going
to be via close contact

405
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:16,920
with respiratory
droplets. And that

406
00:25:16,920 --> 00:25:19,640
type of transmission
can occur year-round.

407
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:20,740
However,

408
00:25:21,140 --> 00:25:24,440
coronavirus has
recently become much

409
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:27,500
more seasonal, almost
similar to the flu.

410
00:25:28,680 --> 00:25:31,660
Now, the virus infects
human cells primarily by

411
00:25:31,660 --> 00:25:35,300
binding to a receptor
called the ACE2 receptor,

412
00:25:35,300 --> 00:25:39,360
the angiotensin-converting
enzyme-2 receptor.

413
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:43,080
And these receptors are
found on surfaces of

414
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:46,680
cells in many tissues
throughout the body, most

415
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:50,180
notably the nasal passage,
throat, and the lungs.

416
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:55,500
Now, the virus docks with
the receptor, and at that

417
00:25:55,500 --> 00:25:58,760
point, a host cell protease
is going to cleave the spike

418
00:25:58,760 --> 00:26:02,560
protein and that allows
membrane fusion with the

419
00:26:02,560 --> 00:26:06,180
host cell and then ultimately
allows for a viral entry.

420
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:13,700
Clinical features of COVID
-19 can range anywhere

421
00:26:13,700 --> 00:26:17,500
from an asymptomatic
infection to critical

422
00:26:17,500 --> 00:26:21,680
respiratory failure with
generalized symptoms typically

423
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:24,460
appearing within about
two weeks of exposure.

424
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:31,100
Now, COVID complications can
range from acute systemic

425
00:26:31,100 --> 00:26:34,820
issues like ARDS, acute
respiratory distress

426
00:26:34,820 --> 00:26:38,820
syndrome, where the virus
triggers inflammation and

427
00:26:38,820 --> 00:26:42,340
causes alveoli in the lungs
to fill up with fluid,

428
00:26:42,340 --> 00:26:47,160
to more chronic issues
like long COVID that can

429
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:51,120
affect various organ systems
for months or even years

430
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:53,920
after infection where
patients can experience

431
00:26:53,920 --> 00:26:58,120
symptoms like chronic fatigue
or even mental changes.

432
00:26:58,420 --> 00:27:02,560
A diagnosis of COVID-19
is primarily accomplished

433
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:06,640
using antigen detection
tests like at-home testing

434
00:27:06,640 --> 00:27:11,000
kits or can to be done by
molecular testing with PCR.

435
00:27:11,500 --> 00:27:15,440
And if infection is
confirmed, then treatment is

436
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:19,780
usually just supportive
along with some antivirals.

437
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:23,700
And in terms of prevention,
the most effective

438
00:27:23,700 --> 00:27:25,620
means is going to be
through vaccination.

439
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:26,300
So

440
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:43,080
that concludes the
lecture. I just wanted

441
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:45,200
to thank everyone
for listening, and I

442
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:47,260
wanted to provide the
link for feedback.